"Just Another Day" is an uptempo, [[pop music|pop]] and [[soul music|soul]], romantic [[sentimental ballad|ballad]]{{efn|"mid-to-fast tempo",{{sfn|Atilano|2017}} uptempo pop,{{sfn|Riccio|1992|p=21}} uptempo pop and soul,{{sfn|Lannert|1992a}} soul,{{sfn|Anon.|1992ah|p=16}} ballad,{{sfn|Tarradell|1992|p=66}} a romantic pop.{{sfn|Larkin|2011}}}} that is characterized by its lively, skipping rhythm and [[Adult contemporary music|adult contemporary]] (AC)-and-[[R&B]], metallic, shuffle beats.{{efn|lively skipping rhythm,{{sfn|Lannert|1992a}}{{sfn|Burr|2004|p=600}} metallic shuffles of adult contemporary and R&B beats.{{sfn|Burr|2004|p=600}}}} It has [[Latin music]] influences{{sfn|Atilano|2017}}{{sfn|Lannert|1992b|p=12F}} and contains pulsating [[house music]] rhythms that broaden its appeal across multiple formats.{{sfn|Campbell|1992|p=13}} "Just Another Day" narrates a melancholic tale of lost love.{{sfn|Lannert|1992b|p=12F}} The track is constructed under a catchy [[hook]],{{sfn|Tarradell|1992|p=66}} and is laced with a seductive [[Soul II Soul]]-like, rhythmic cadence in which Secada sings, "I don't want to find another way to make it through the day without you",{{sfn|Morse|1992|p=9}} capturing the lyrics' story of love's sporadic nature,{{sfn|Riccio|1992|p=21}} in a baritone-[[tenor]] voice.{{sfn|Pitts, Jr.|1992|p=5}} ''[[The Palm Beach Post]]'' called the track, along with "If You Go" and "Angel", an integration of Secada's Latin rhythms with American pop-and-R&B tones.{{sfn|Anon.|2007|p=46}} This is echoed by Deborah Davis of ''[[El Norte (Monterrey)|El Norte]]'', who found the track to be a mixture of R&B, and the sound and passion of Latin music.{{sfn|Davis|1996|p=12}} Raquel Riaño of ''[[El Confidencial]]'' called the track a catchy, romantic pop song{{sfn|Riaño|2021}} and María Ivette Vega Calles of ''[[El Nuevo Dia]]'' called it an "alternative ballad".{{sfn|Vega Calles|2017}}

[[Stephen Holden]] of ''[[The New York Times]]'' lauded Secada's ability to transform a "lovelorn ballad" into an impactful song by infusing it with a driving beat, and said while Secada's intense vocal performance lends the song authenticity and credibility.{{sfn|Holden|1994}} Ricard Riccio of ''[[St. Petersburg Times]]'' lauded "Just Another Day" as an exceptional, rejuvenating pop song and commended Emilio's sound production but criticized Gloria's backing vocals for failing to prevent the overall blandness of the songs on the album.{{sfn|Riccio|1992|p=21}} ''[[Waterloo Region Record]]'' deemed the Spanish version to be an improvement over the English version, applauding Secada's emotive vocals that are set against minor keys and unpredictable melodies. This combination, according to the review, results in an almost-anthemic expression of poignant longing.{{sfn|Anon.|1992af|p=C17}} ''Billboard''{{'}}s [[Larry Flick]] praised Gloria's harmonies but found Secada had charismatic presence as a contender for top-40 and AC radio. Flick said the production was well-executed and blends a rhythmic, slow [[Groove (music)|groove]] containing captivating piano melodies and suitably dramatic vocal performance. Flick called "Just Another Day" a great introduction to Secada, noticing his potential for a successful career.{{sfn|Flick|1992|p=82}}

In the song's lyrics, the protagonist desperately clings to a relationship, hoping his partner's inconsistent behavior will change. Expressing his emotions, he says without her, "it's just another day".{{sfn|Martin|1992|p=11}} Charlie Martin of ''[[The Messenger (Mount Airy, North Carolina)|The Messenger]]'' said the song's message is lacking because it depicts a person who is constantly depressed due to the unpredictable presence of his lover.{{sfn|Martin|1992|p=11}} According to Joseph Atilano of [[Inquirer.net]], the song has a lively beat that resembles a dance-floor anthem and the lyrics have a poignant impact, particularly resonating with the broken-hearted and lonely. Atilano also said Secada expresses his deep emotions for the woman he loves, despite her indifference, leaving him feeling lost and adrift without her love.{{sfn|Atilano|2017}}

